Since the **Resources** relate to the growing list of 3D data formats, theoretically they can be anything but the name tree string must be compatible with ECMAScript (as already stated). Therefore basic objects such as integers, reals, booleans, and strings would all seem to be OK. Streams should also be OK (since it is really the stream data). However arrays and dictionaries are expressed in PDF syntax and that is not necessarily appropriate for the 3D data format, if they even support such data structures - and if they did it would be better if these were encoded as strings using the syntax appropriate for the 3D data format (e.g. adding commas between array elements, using JSON format, etc).

So the question becomes: does the PDF stipulate an allowed set (and thus a prohibited set) like is currently done for DPM; or does it allow any PDF object along with an "implementation dependent" statement? Reliable interoperability across 3D runtimes probably needs the former along with some application/implementation notes for each 3D format.

